#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0392894c-6bbc-544a-8ab5-c0d2be332967" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Missing Number in a Pascal's Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "4014e57c-3c50-5358-a9ae-280610aa6cfb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5107" :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & ◻\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\\begin{vmatrix}⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1$. The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $1$ and $3$.\n\n$$\n1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & ◻\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\\begin{vmatrix}⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $1$ and $3$:\n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = 1 + 3 = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f386de30-38b8-59b7-a6e8-ea5e386671d5" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "57c2e733-51b4-51f5-8774-ebbd6da2321d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5152" :question/problem "Expand the binomial $(2z + 1)^4$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n\n$$\n\nWe have that $n=4$, $a = 2z$, and $b= 1$. So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n$a^4b^0,\\quad a^{3}b^1,\\quad a^{2}b^2,\\quad a^1b^3,\\quad a^0b^4$.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (2z + 1)^4 &=\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4 \\\\[5pt] &=\\mathbf{1}(2z)^4(1)^0 + \\mathbf{4}(2z)^3(1)^1 + \\mathbf{6}(2z)^2(1)^2 + \\mathbf{4}(2z)^1(1)^3 + \\mathbf{1}(2z)^0(1)^4 \\\\[5pt] &=(2z)^4 + 4(2z)^3 + 6(2z)^2 + 4(2z) +1 \\\\[5pt] &=16z^4 + 32z^3 + 24z^2 + 8z + 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "bc866f37-17b6-59e0-9088-fd71a46c5b9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5158" :question/problem "Expand the binomial $(3x-y)^4$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4$, $a = 3x$, and $b= -y$. So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n$a^4b^0,\\quad a^{3}b^1,\\quad a^{2}b^2,\\quad a^1b^3,\\quad a^0b^4$.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (3x - y)^4 &=\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4 \\\\[5pt] &= \\mathbf{1}(3x)^4(-y)^0 + \\mathbf{4}(3x)^3(-y)^1 + \\mathbf{6}(3x)^2(-y)^2 + \\mathbf{4}(3x)^1(-y)^3 + \\mathbf{1}(3x)^0(-y)^4 \\\\[5pt] &= \\mathbf{1}\\left(81x^4\\right)(1) + \\mathbf{4}\\left(27x^3\\right)(-y) + \\mathbf{6}\\left(9x^2\\right)(y^2) + \\mathbf{4}(3x)(-y^3) + \\mathbf{1}(1)(y^4) \\\\[5pt] &= 81x^4 - 108x^3y + 54x^2y^2 - 12xy^3 + y^4. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/id #uuid "b3546d6e-8399-5694-85db-c73f0a57b95c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5159" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of ${(2x^2+y)}^3$." :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=3$, we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n\\left(2x^2\\right)^3y^0, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\left(2x^2\\right)^2 y^1}},\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^1 y^2,\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^0 y^3\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns $x^4y$ is the second. So, we are looking for the second term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,{\\color{red}3},3,1)$. Therefore, the second term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2x^{2})^{2}(y)^{1} &= 3(4x^{4})(y) \\\\\n&= 12x^{4}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of ${(2x^2+y)}^3$ is $12$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f8f548-1a49-5e89-92e8-7aa6e15f79f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098038} {:answer/id #uuid "b7101996-4e35-5131-bd5e-49935795be88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098039} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5d0997-0855-5ead-8f10-8018708134ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098040} {:answer/id #uuid "e3de5239-b152-58fc-a20d-335a98123085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098041} {:answer/id #uuid "1a11a0cb-3075-5071-a3f1-76be246ed14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098042}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef447e9-f097-5387-9108-98c2445f35bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098037}] :question/id #uuid "dfb7c79b-ea9c-5c43-a6d6-7b5de5669f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12875" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$(y + x^{2})^{3} = y^{3} + Ay^{2}x^{2} + Byx^{4} + Cx^{6}$\n\nwhere $A, B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A + B + C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f386de30-38b8-59b7-a6e8-ea5e386671d5" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "945fcc9e-b2f2-570b-b412-c02b97375958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 160n + 300n^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099562} {:answer/id #uuid "a292be4f-bc01-5068-a805-7858c4263b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 80n + 600n^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099563} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ed2a5f-53fb-576d-a952-132ae901413d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 160n + 350n^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099564} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9d9cdf-b709-53cc-b0e1-9bd0c7904a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 160n + 600n^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099565} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab09a95-f39d-5e6b-80eb-7ca2d2517ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 80n + 300n^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "00fe569c-7704-57d0-bfbe-6b9477f1dab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099561}] :question/id #uuid "43307ab9-268b-531